---
import Try from "../Try.astro";
import "../sternzeit/sternzeit.css";
import "./dunstkreis.css";
import "./distance.css";
---

<figure class="scene-frame distance" id="distance">
    <div class="sky-view distance-view as-seen">
        <canvas data-field="canvas" aria-label="Four ridges one behind another, faded by the air between"></canvas>
        <div class="distance-labels" data-field="labels" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="distance-profile"><svg aria-hidden="true"></svg></div>
    <label class="figure-slider" title="How far the nearest ridge is; each one behind is twice as far">
        <span class="note">Nearest ridge</span>
        <input data-field="distance" type="range" min="0" max="4" step="0.01" value="2" />
        <output data-field="distanceValue"></output>
    </label>
    <figcaption class="figure-caption">
        Four ridges, 200, 500, 1200 and 3000 m high, each twice as far as the one in front, seen through the air between
        them and the eye: the light it adds and the light of the ridges it takes away, from dunstkreis' aerial
        perspective volume, looked up at the length of each pixel's ray. Under it, the same from the side, heights
        exaggerated: the dotted level through the eye, the ground curving away below it, by how much at the farthest
        ridge, and the sight lines to the tops, dashed where something hides one. The view stays put, so ridges moved
        out shrink and sink: Earth's curvature lowers the farthest by 0.6° at 128 km, and the air's bending gives a
        fifth of that back.
        <Try label="Hazy noon" set="date=2026-06-21T11:00:00Z&lat=52.395&lon=13.085&h=1" fig="distance">
            Move the nearest ridge out to 16 km: the farthest, 128 km away, all but fades into the sky behind it.
        </Try>
    </figcaption>
</figure>

<script>
    import "./distance";
</script>
